---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import MoreArticles from "../../components/MoreArticles.astro";
import { essays, plainTitle, essaySchema, shareImage } from "../../data/essays.js";
const slug = Astro.url.pathname.replace(/\/$/, '').split('/').at(-1);
const essay = essays.find(e => e.slug === slug);
if (!essay) throw new Error(`No essay data found for slug: ${slug}`);
const site = import.meta.env.SITE;
const schema = essaySchema(essay, site);
---

<Base title={`${plainTitle(essay.title)} · uxward`} description={essay.deck} schema={schema} ogType="article" ogImage={shareImage(essay)} bodyClass="essay">
  <div class="read-bar" id="read-bar" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="reading-column essay-top">
    <div class="row">
      <a class="back" href="/writing">Back to writing</a>
      <div class="meta">
        <span>{essay.quarter}</span>
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <span>Essay</span>
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <span>9 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="reading-column essay-title-block">
    <h1 class="essay-title" set:html={essay.title} />
    <p class="essay-deck">{essay.deck}</p>
    <div class="essay-byline">By Brandon E. B. Ward</div>
  </div>

  <article class="reading-column essay-body">
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/writing/six-million-dollar-man.webp" width="1456" height="819"
        alt="Lee Majors as Steve Austin, the Six Million Dollar Man."
      />
      <figcaption>
        We can rebuild the team. We have the technology. Ref: <a target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          href="https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0071054/">The Six Million Dollar Man</a
        >
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="series-nav series-nav-top">
      <a href="/writing/the-medium-is-the-message-part-i-we-never-had-a-medium"
        >Part I</a
      > · <a
        href="/writing/the-medium-is-the-message-part-ii-turtles-all-the-way-down"
        >Part II</a
      > · <a
        href="/writing/the-medium-is-the-message-part-iii-the-medium-doesnt-strike-back"
        >Part III</a
      > · Part IV
    </div>

    <p>
      Let me go back to the project I opened this series with, and this time tell
      the part I skipped.
    </p>
    <p>
      We built the website without Figma. We designed by making the real, running
      thing. And then, as I mentioned almost in passing at the start, we handed
      that working site to a CMS developer, who took it apart and rebuilt it
      inside the content system the client would actually use, so they could own
      and maintain it themselves.
    </p>
    <p>
      So yeah, it's a handoff. A translation from one medium into another.
      Another turtle. After four parts arguing that we should collapse the
      distance between designing a thing and building it, we designed the thing,
      built it, and then passed it to someone who rebuilt it somewhere else.
    </p>
    <p>
      That looks like a contradiction of everything I've said. It's actually the
      whole point, and I'll earn that claim by the end. First, why the team was
      ever shaped this way.
    </p>

    <h2>The roles were never natural</h2>
    <p>
      UX designer. UI designer. Front-end developer. We treat these the way we
      treat elements on the periodic table, as if software had always required
      exactly these divisions, as if they'd been discovered rather than invented.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/writing/periodic-table-of-software-development.jpg" width="1456" height="898"
        alt="A periodic-table-style chart of software development roles, disciplines, and practices."
      />
      <figcaption>
        View an interactive version here: <a target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          href="https://precocity.github.io/periodic-table"
          >Periodic Table of Software Development</a
        >
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>They were inherited.</p>
    <p>
      McLuhan's argument about the printing press is that the mechanical age took
      whole activities and shattered them into fragments: specialists, linear
      steps, separated functions. The assembly line is that same logic pointed at
      making things, each worker doing one small piece and passing it down.
      Fragmentation and specialism were the <em>message</em> of the mechanical age,
      no matter what it happened to be producing. Our discipline was born inside that
      logic and never thought to question it. Research over here, wireframes over
      there, visual design next, a handoff to engineering at the end. A relay of
      specialists, each polishing one fragment and sliding it to the next station.
    </p>

    <h2>Software comes out shaped like the org chart</h2>
    <p>
      In 1968, a programmer named <a target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melvin_Conway">Melvin Conway</a
      > noticed something that has haunted software ever since. Organizations, he
      argued, are constrained to build systems that mirror their own communication
      structure. The classic illustration: put four teams on a compiler and you'll
      get a four-pass compiler. The shape of the group becomes the shape of the thing.
      We call it <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conway%27s_law"
        >Conway's Law</a
      >.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/writing/conways-law.webp" width="1456" height="971"
        alt="A diagram showing a four-team org chart on the left mirrored by a four-module system architecture on the right."
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      Point it at design and it stings. A team split into separate specialists,
      each in a separate tool, joined only by handoffs, produces work with the
      seams of those handoffs showing. The gap between the research and the
      interface, between the interface and the build, between the designed intent
      and the shipped reality: those gaps are the org chart, made visible in the
      product. You've felt them as a user. That slightly-off place where a
      beautiful screen meets a clunky flow is very often the spot where one
      specialist handed off to another.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the relay existed, and why it's dissolving</h2>
    <p>
      The relay wasn't only habit. It had a hard structural reason underneath it,
      the one this entire series has been circling. The designer <em
        >could not build the thing</em
      >. They worked in a tool that made pictures of software, so someone else
      had to translate the pictures into the real material. The handoff wasn't a
      preference or a sign of maturity. It was forced, because design and build
      happened in two different media.
    </p>
    <p>
      Take that constraint away and the justification goes with it. When you can
      shape the running product directly, the wall between the person who designs
      it and the person who makes it real stops being a law of physics and
      becomes a choice. Usually a choice made out of habit, because the old shape
      is still sitting there.
    </p>
    <p>
      McLuhan saw this reversal coming. Automation, he argued, runs the opposite
      direction from the assembly line. Where the mechanical age fragmented and
      separated, the automated, information-saturated age is integral and
      decentralized: it dissolves the narrow "job," the single repeated task on
      the line, and asks instead for a "role," which pulls a person into the
      whole. And that's exactly what happens when you make in the medium. You
      cannot cleanly separate the UX from the UI from the front-end from the
      back-end and all their connective tissues when all are the same living
      thing moving under your hands. Those separations were artifacts of a world
      where they were literally different files, in different tools, owned by
      different people. Collapse the tools into one medium and the roles want to
      collapse too.
    </p>

    <h2>The catch that the tool vendors won't mention</h2>
    <p>
      Conway's Law cuts both ways, and this is the sentence to tape to your
      monitor: if you adopt the new medium and keep the old org, the org wins.
    </p>
    <p>
      You'll use AI to generate nicer representations faster, and then throw them
      over the very same walls, to the very same specialists, through the very
      same handoffs. The seams will still show, because the communication
      structure that produces the seams is still there. New medium, old org, same
      seams. The tool does not reshape the team. Only you can do that, and only
      on purpose.
    </p>
    <p>
      Which is the answer to the question the last piece left hanging. Staying
      engaged and aware in a frictionless medium can't be a heroic act by one
      designer surrounded by people on autopilot. It has to be built into the
      shape of the team: fewer people, each involved in more of the whole, each
      able to see and touch the living thing, each responsible for keeping it
      honest rather than for buffing one fragment and passing it along. Awareness
      isn't a task you assign. It's a structure you build.
    </p>

    <h2>Back to the turtle in my own project</h2>
    <p>
      So, the Craft CMS developer. The handoff made after all my talk about
      killing handoffs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here's the honest version. We did collapse most of the relay. There was no
      research-to-wireframe-to-visual-to-prototype-to-spec-to-engineering chain,
      because we designed by building the real thing. But at the very end, we
      handed the working site to a developer to rebuild inside a content system,
      because the client has to live with this site long after we're gone, and
      they need it in a medium they can operate without us. That last translation
      wasn't a failure to honor the thesis. It <em>was</em> the thesis, applied with
      judgment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because the goal was never zero handoffs. That's a fantasy, and this series
      began by refusing fantasies of arrival. Software is never done (until it's
      deleted). Software gets built, owned, and maintained by different people,
      in different media, across the years. Pretending otherwise is just going
      numb. The goal is fewer translation steps, and honesty about those that
      remain: which medium each person works in, which medium each person will
      maintain, and why. We didn't reach the bottom of the stack. Nobody does. We
      chose a better altitude than the old process would ever have allowed, kept
      one deliberate handoff because the client truly needed it, and said so out
      loud instead of pretending it wasn't there.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fewer turtles. Or more turtles depending on how you think of it. And those
      that remain, chosen on purpose.
    </p>
    <p>
      There's a quieter reward in this. The fragmented job, one specialist
      buffing one fragment, was never where the work felt most alive. The
      involved role, one person shaping the whole living thing, sits much closer
      to the deep engagement, the flow Csikszentmihalyi called our best state.
      Re-fusing the team does more than speed things up. It's more awake, and
      more human, for the people doing it. Which matters more than it sounds,
      because the people delivering the work are those who have to believe in it.
    </p>

    <h2>Make the message in its medium</h2>
    <p>AI didn't upgrade our tools. It handed us our medium.</p>
    <p>
      We are no longer drawing the future and praying it survives the trip to
      code. We can build it in the material it's actually made of. And when the
      medium is the message, we owe it to both to make our message <em>in</em> its
      medium. A delivery team that does <em>that</em> is a different thing, not a
      faster version of the old one, and we are the ones who get to design it.
    </p>
    <p>One last honesty, the one I promised in the very first piece.</p>
    <p>
      When the next medium arrives, the one after this, someone will notice that
      what we're calling the real thing was itself just another layer, and
      they'll write this essay again about a rung none of us can see from here.
      They'll be right. It was true of the punch card and the compiler and the
      graphical interface, and it's true of this. That isn't a caveat, and it
      isn't a hedge. It's the job. We were never going to reach the bottom.
    </p>
    <p>
      We just get to choose, for our moment, how honestly we stand on the turtle
      we've got.
    </p>
    <p>The end (for now).</p>


    <h2>Works referenced</h2>
    <p>
